{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Baumeister1967-Fig15-single-tunnel-absorbing",
  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Optical Tunneling and Its Applications to Optical Filters,\" Appl. Opt. 6, 897-904 (1967). doi:10.1364/AO.6.000897 - Table III, columns 'Figs. 13-15' and footnote (p. 902); Fig. 15 caption, text p. 903",
  "Notes": "Single tunnel layer bandpass filter of Fig. 13 with a slightly absorbing tunnel layer (1.35 - j0.01)\nSpecification: As Fig. 13; illustrates the effect of absorption in the tunnel layer.\nReported: Text p. 903: the peak transmittance (p polarization) is reduced to 84 % if the tunnel layer is slightly absorbing. Fig. 15: dashed (absorbing) p curve peaks ~0.84-0.86 near lambda0/lambda ~0.97-1.0, otherwise close to the lossless curve (~0.2 at 0.9, ~0.07 at 1.1).\nNotes: Same stack as Baumeister1967-Fig13-single-tunnel (including the corrections of printed layers 9 -> 1.302 and 18 -> 2.000, see that design's notes) with layer 11 = 1.35 - j0.01 (Table III footnote: 'For Fig. 15, the optical constant of layer 11 is n = n - jk = 1.35 - j0.01'). Own TMM check: p peak 0.866 at lambda0/lambda = 0.991 (paper 84 %).\nLayers from Table III, columns 'Figs. 13-15' and footnote (p. 902); Fig. 15 caption, text p. 903; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours p peak T 86.6 %; above 90 % of it from lambda0/lambda 0.957 to 1.006; paper p peak 84 % (p. 903). The absorbing layer’s k is read from the text; 2.6 points high.",
